 "lq": [
  {
   "_id": 32005,
   "ANSWER": "<p style=\"margin-bottom:11px\">&nbsp;</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-bottom:11px\"><span style=\"font-size:12pt\"><span style=\"line-height:115%\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><b>1. Introduction</b></span></span></span></p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-bottom:11px\"><span style=\"font-size:12pt\"><span style=\"line-height:115%\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><b>The Intel 8086 is a 16-bit microprocessor introduced by Intel in 1978. It marked a major advancement in microprocessor design, serving as the foundation for the x86 family of processors. It is capable of addressing 1 MB of memory and supports multiprogramming, modular design, and efficient instruction execution.</b></span></span></span></p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-bottom:11px\">&nbsp;</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-bottom:11px\"><span style=\"font-size:12pt\"><span style=\"line-height:115%\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><b>2. Architecture of 8086</b></span></span></span></p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-bottom:11px\"><span style=\"font-size:12pt\"><span style=\"line-height:115%\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><b>The 8086 architecture is divided into two main functional units:</b></span></span></span></p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-bottom:11px\"><span style=\"font-size:12pt\"><span style=\"line-height:115%\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><b>(A) Bus Interface Unit (BIU)</b></span></span></span></p>\n\n<ul>\n\t<li style=\"margin-bottom:11px\"><span style=\"font-size:12pt\"><span style=\"tab-stops:list 36.0pt\"><span style=\"line-height:115%\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><b>Handles all data and address transfers between the processor and memory/IO.</b></span></span></span></span></li>\n\t<li style=\"margin-bottom:11px\"><span style=\"font-size:12pt\"><span style=\"tab-stops:list 36.0pt\"><span style=\"line-height:115%\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><b>Functions:</b></span></span></span></span>\n\t<ul style=\"list-style-type:circle\">\n\t\t<li style=\"margin-bottom:11px\"><span style=\"font-size:12pt\"><span style=\"tab-stops:list 72.0pt\"><span style=\"line-height:115%\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><b>Instruction fetching from memory.</b></span></span></span></span></li>\n\t\t<li style=\"margin-bottom:11px\"><span style=\"font-size:12pt\"><span style=\"tab-stops:list 72.0pt\"><span style=\"line-height:115%\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><b>Queuing instructions in a 6-byte instruction queue (pipelining).</b></span></span></span></span></li>\n\t\t<li style=\"margin-bottom:11px\"><span style=\"font-size:12pt\"><span style=\"tab-stops:list 72.0pt\"><span style=\"line-height:115%\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><b>Segment and physical address calculation.</b></span></span></span></span></li>\n\t</ul>\n\t</li>\n\t<li style=\"margin-bottom:11px\"><span style=\"font-size:12pt\"><span style=\"tab-stops:list 36.0pt\"><span style=\"line-height:115%\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><b>Components:</b></span></span></span></span>\n\t<ul style=\"list-style-type:circle\">\n\t\t<li style=\"margin-bottom:11px\"><span style=\"font-size:12pt\"><span style=\"tab-stops:list 72.0pt\"><span style=\"line-height:115%\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><b>Segment Registers: CS (Code), DS (Data), SS (Stack), ES (Extra).</b></span></span></span></span></li>\n\t\t<li style=\"margin-bottom:11px\"><span style=\"font-size:12pt\"><span style=\"tab-stops:list 72.0pt\"><span style=\"line-height:115%\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><b>Instruction Pointer (IP): Holds the offset of the next instruction.</b></span></span></span></span></li>\n\t\t<li style=\"margin-bottom:11px\"><span style=\"font-size:12pt\"><span style=\"tab-stops:list 72.0pt\"><span style=\"line-height:115%\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><b>Address Generation Circuitry.</b></span></span></span></span></li>\n\t</ul>\n\t</li>\n</ul>\n\n<p style=\"margin-bottom:11px\"><span style=\"font-size:12pt\"><span style=\"line-height:115%\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><b>(B) Execution Unit (EU)</b></span></span></span></p>\n\n<ul>\n\t<li style=\"margin-bottom:11px\"><span style=\"font-size:12pt\"><span style=\"tab-stops:list 36.0pt\"><span style=\"line-height:115%\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><b>Executes instructions fetched by the BIU.</b></span></span></span></span></li>\n\t<li style=\"margin-bottom:11px\"><span style=\"font-size:12pt\"><span style=\"tab-stops:list 36.0pt\"><span style=\"line-height:115%\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><b>Functions:</b></span></span></span></span>\n\t<ul style=\"list-style-type:circle\">\n\t\t<li style=\"margin-bottom:11px\"><span style=\"font-size:12pt\"><span style=\"tab-stops:list 72.0pt\"><span style=\"line-height:115%\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><b>Decoding instructions.</b></span></span></span></span></li>\n\t\t<li style=\"margin-bottom:11px\"><span style=\"font-size:12pt\"><span style=\"tab-stops:list 72.0pt\"><span style=\"line-height:115%\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><b>Performing arithmetic and logical operations.</b></span></span></span></span></li>\n\t\t<li style=\"margin-bottom:11px\"><span style=\"font-size:12pt\"><span style=\"tab-stops:list 72.0pt\"><span style=\"line-height:115%\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><b>Controlling program flow.</b></span></span></span></span></li>\n\t</ul>\n\t</li>\n\t<li style=\"margin-bottom:11px\"><span style=\"font-size:12pt\"><span style=\"tab-stops:list 36.0pt\"><span style=\"line-height:115%\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><b>Components:</b></span></span></span></span>\n\t<ul style=\"list-style-type:circle\">\n\t\t<li style=\"margin-bottom:11px\"><span style=\"font-size:12pt\"><span style=\"tab-stops:list 72.0pt\"><span style=\"line-height:115%\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><b>ALU (Arithmetic Logic Unit) &ndash; Performs arithmetic and logical operations.</b></span></span></span></span></li>\n\t\t<li style=\"margin-bottom:11px\"><span style=\"font-size:12pt\"><span style=\"tab-stops:list 72.0pt\"><span style=\"line-height:115%\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><b>General Purpose Registers: AX, BX, CX, DX (can be divided into 8-bit halves: AH/AL, BH/BL, etc.).</b></span></span></span></span></li>\n\t\t<li style=\"margin-bottom:11px\"><span style=\"font-size:12pt\"><span style=\"tab-stops:list 72.0pt\"><span style=\"line-height:115%\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><b>Pointer and Index Registers: SP, BP, SI, DI.</b></span></span></span></span></li>\n\t\t<li style=\"margin-bottom:11px\"><span style=\"font-size:12pt\"><span style=\"tab-stops:list 72.0pt\"><span style=\"line-height:115%\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><b>Flags Register: Holds status flags (Zero, Carry, Sign, Parity, Auxiliary Carry, Overflow, etc.) and control flags (Direction, Interrupt Enable, Trap).</b></span></span></span></span></li>\n\t</ul>\n\t</li>\n</ul>\n\n<p style=\"margin-bottom:11px\"><span style=\"font-size:12pt\"><span style=\"line-height:115%\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><b>Pipelining: While the EU executes one instruction, the BIU pre-fetches the next instruction, improving execution speed.</b></span></span></span></p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-bottom:11px\">&nbsp;</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-bottom:11px\"><span style=\"font-size:12pt\"><span style=\"line-height:115%\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><b>3. Key Features of 8086</b></span></span></span></p>\n\n<ol>\n\t<li style=\"margin-bottom:11px\"><span style=\"font-size:12pt\"><span style=\"tab-stops:list 36.0pt\"><span style=\"line-height:115%\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><b>Word Length: 16-bit processor (can process 16-bit data at a time).</b></span></span></span></span></li>\n\t<li style=\"margin-bottom:11px\"><span style=\"font-size:12pt\"><span style=\"tab-stops:list 36.0pt\"><span style=\"line-height:115%\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><b>Memory Addressing: 20-bit address bus &rarr; can address 1 MB of memory.</b></span></span></span></span></li>\n\t<li style=\"margin-bottom:11px\"><span style=\"font-size:12pt\"><span style=\"tab-stops:list 36.0pt\"><span style=\"line-height:115%\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><b>Registers:</b></span></span></span></span>\n\t<ul style=\"list-style-type:circle\">\n\t\t<li style=\"margin-bottom:11px\"><span style=\"font-size:12pt\"><span style=\"tab-stops:list 72.0pt\"><span style=\"line-height:115%\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><b>General-purpose (AX, BX, CX, DX).</b></span></span></span></span></li>\n\t\t<li style=\"margin-bottom:11px\"><span style=\"font-size:12pt\"><span style=\"tab-stops:list 72.0pt\"><span style=\"line-height:115%\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><b>Segment registers (CS, DS, SS, ES).</b></span></span></span></span></li>\n\t\t<li style=\"margin-bottom:11px\"><span style=\"font-size:12pt\"><span style=\"tab-stops:list 72.0pt\"><span style=\"line-height:115%\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><b>Special-purpose (IP, SP, BP, SI, DI).</b></span></span></span></span></li>\n\t\t<li style=\"margin-bottom:11px\"><span style=\"font-size:12pt\"><span style=\"tab-stops:list 72.0pt\"><span style=\"line-height:115%\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><b>Flags register (status + control).</b></span></span></span></span></li>\n\t</ul>\n\t</li>\n\t<li style=\"margin-bottom:11px\"><span style=\"font-size:12pt\"><span style=\"tab-stops:list 36.0pt\"><span style=\"line-height:115%\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><b>Instruction Queue (Pipelining): 6-byte instruction queue enables faster execution.</b></span></span></span></span></li>\n\t<li style=\"margin-bottom:11px\"><span style=\"font-size:12pt\"><span style=\"tab-stops:list 36.0pt\"><span style=\"line-height:115%\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><b>Operating Modes:</b></span></span></span></span>\n\t<ul style=\"list-style-type:circle\">\n\t\t<li style=\"margin-bottom:11px\"><span style=\"font-size:12pt\"><span style=\"tab-stops:list 72.0pt\"><span style=\"line-height:115%\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><b>Minimum Mode &ndash; single processor environment.</b></span></span></span></span></li>\n\t\t<li style=\"margin-bottom:11px\"><span style=\"font-size:12pt\"><span style=\"tab-stops:list 72.0pt\"><span style=\"line-height:115%\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><b>Maximum Mode &ndash; multiprocessor environment (with coprocessors like 8087).</b></span></span></span></span></li>\n\t</ul>\n\t</li>\n\t<li style=\"margin-bottom:11px\"><span style=\"font-size:12pt\"><span style=\"tab-stops:list 36.0pt\"><span style=\"line-height:115%\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><b>Instruction Set:</b></span></span></span></span>\n\t<ul style=\"list-style-type:circle\">\n\t\t<li style=\"margin-bottom:11px\"><span style=\"font-size:12pt\"><span style=\"tab-stops:list 72.0pt\"><span style=\"line-height:115%\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><b>Rich set including data transfer, arithmetic, logic, control transfer, string operations, and bit manipulation.</b></span></span></span></span></li>\n\t</ul>\n\t</li>\n\t<li style=\"margin-bottom:11px\"><span style=\"font-size:12pt\"><span style=\"tab-stops:list 36.0pt\"><span style=\"line-height:115%\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><b>Clock Speed: Typically 5&ndash;10 MHz.</b></span></span></span></span></li>\n\t<li style=\"margin-bottom:11px\"><span style=\"font-size:12pt\"><span style=\"tab-stops:list 36.0pt\"><span style=\"line-height:115%\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><b>I/O Handling: Supports both memory-mapped and I/O-mapped devices.</b></span></span></span></span></li>\n\t<li style=\"margin-bottom:11px\"><span style=\"font-size:12pt\"><span style=\"tab-stops:list 36.0pt\"><span style=\"line-height:115%\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><b>Compatibility: Basis for later x86 processors, ensuring backward compatibility.</b></span></span></span></span></li>\n</ol>\n\n<p style=\"margin-bottom:11px\">&nbsp;</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-bottom:11px\">&nbsp;</p>\n",
